<% content_for :title, "Hinduism & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="hinduism-addiction-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="hinduism-addiction-hero">
    <picture class="hinduism-addiction-background">
      <source media="(max-width: 767px)"
              srcset="<%= asset_path('addiction_hinduism_mobile.png') %>">
      <%= image_tag "addiction_hinduism_desktop.png",
          alt: "",
          class: "hinduism-addiction-background-image",
          fetchpriority: "high" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="hinduism-addiction-hero-content">
      <p class="hinduism-addiction-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / PERSPECTIVES / HINDUISM
      </p>
      <h1>
        <span>ADDICTION</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>HINDU</span>
        <span>LENS.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="hinduism-addiction-intro">
        KNOW WHAT GUIDES YOU.<br>
        ACT WITH CARE.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 / OPENING -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-pink" id="another-way-to-look">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">01 / ANOTHER WAY TO LOOK</p>
      <h2>MORE<br>TO<br>UNDERSTAND.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <blockquote>
        “The mind can be the friend and also the enemy of the self.”
        <cite>— Bhagavad Gita 6.5 (paraphrase)</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="ha-lead">
        Addiction can be examined through desire, attachment, karma, dharma,
        and the relationship between the mind and the Self.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hindu philosophy recognizes desire as part of life, but becoming attached
        to satisfying it can cloud your judgment. Alcohol may promise relief from
        physical pain, mental restlessness, or emotional distress. The relief can
        be real and temporary, while the consequences of your decisions extend
        far beyond it. These teachings offer questions about what drives you and
        what responsibilities remain when a desire becomes overwhelming.
      </p>
      <div class="ha-callout">
        DESIRE IS REAL.<br>
        SO ARE CONSEQUENCES.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 / IDENTITY -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-yellow" id="self-and-identity">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">02 / THE SELF &amp; IDENTITY</p>
      <h2>WHO<br>ARE<br>YOU?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p class="ha-lead">
        Your cravings, intoxication, emotions, and past actions are not the entirety of who you are.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Upanishads explore <em>Atman</em>, the deeper Self, as distinct from changing
        thoughts and experiences. You can recognize that your behavior sometimes
        conflicted with your values without pretending it belonged to someone else.
        Understanding the conditions that influenced a decision does not erase your
        responsibility for making it.
      </p>
      <div class="ha-grid">
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>YOUR EXPERIENCE</h3>
          <p>Pain, craving, mania, shame, and intoxication can alter how you feel and act.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>YOUR IDENTITY</h3>
          <p>One action doesn't define your whole identity, but it remains yours to acknowledge.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        You can examine what you did without turning your worst decisions into
        a permanent definition of yourself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 / DISCERNMENT -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-purple" id="discernment-and-direction">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">03 / DISCERNMENT &amp; DIRECTION</p>
      <h2>WHAT'S<br>GUIDING<br>YOU?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p class="ha-lead">
        What you want in a moment is not always what serves your life.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <em>Bhagavad Gita</em> describes how dwelling on desired experiences
        can develop into attachment and craving, with consequences for judgment.
        The <em>Katha Upanishad</em> uses a chariot image to explore the relationship
        between the senses, mind, discernment, and Self. Addiction can make the
        immediate promise of alcohol more compelling than the direction you
        actually want to take.
      </p>
      <div class="ha-stack">
        <div class="ha-stack-item">
          <span>01</span>
          <div><h3>NOTICE THE ATTRACTION</h3><p>What is alcohol promising: relief, quiet, pleasure, or reassurance?</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-stack-item">
          <span>02</span>
          <div><h3>CONSIDER THE DIRECTION</h3><p>What happens after the immediate desire has been satisfied?</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-stack-item">
          <span>03</span>
          <div><h3>MAKE ROOM FOR DISCERNMENT</h3><p>What support or preparation helps you act before judgment becomes impaired?</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The three <em>gunas</em> offer another symbolic lens: <em>sattva</em>
        represents clarity, <em>rajas</em> restlessness and desire, and <em>tamas</em>
        inertia and obscurity. They are not medical explanations for bipolar
        disorder or intoxication, but they can help you reflect on the states
        that influence your actions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 / ACTION -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-black" id="action-and-outcomes">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">04 / ACTION &amp; OUTCOMES</p>
      <h2>DO<br>YOUR<br>PART.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p class="ha-lead">
        Your intentions explain something about an action. They do not erase what followed.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Karma</em> concerns action and its consequences. <em>Karma Yoga</em>
        emphasizes fulfilling your responsibilities without becoming attached
        to a particular reward or result. You may have intended to relieve
        suffering through alcohol, but that intention doesn't cancel the harm
        caused by drinking and driving or by sending hurtful messages.
      </p>
      <div class="ha-grid">
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>THE ACTION</h3>
          <p>Acknowledge what you chose to do, including the circumstances that influenced you.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>THE OUTCOME</h3>
          <p>Accept consequences and recognize that repair and forgiveness are not entirely in your control.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        You cannot go back and make a different decision. You can take responsibility,
        make amends where possible, and decide how to act now.
      </p>
      <div class="ha-callout">YOU CANNOT UNDO IT.<br>YOU CAN DO YOUR PART.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 / RESPONSIBILITY -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-pink" id="dharma-and-non-harming">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">05 / DHARMA &amp; NON-HARMING</p>
      <h2>LIVE<br>WITH<br>CARE.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p class="ha-lead">
        <em>Dharma</em> asks you to consider your responsibilities; <em>ahimsa</em>
        asks you to consider the harm your actions cause.
      </p>
      <p>
        In your stories, drinking sometimes began as an attempt to relieve pain
        or distress and ended by affecting other people. You can acknowledge the
        reality of your suffering without treating it as permission to harm others.
        Responsibility can include legal obligations, honest apologies, sobriety,
        following treatment, and respecting another person's right to walk away.
      </p>
      <div class="ha-grid">
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>YOUR RESPONSIBILITIES</h3>
          <p>Own your actions and follow through on your obligations.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>YOUR RELATIONSHIPS</h3>
          <p>Recognize harm, apologize sincerely, and respect other people's choices.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>YOUR WELL-BEING</h3>
          <p>Seek effective care rather than expecting endurance or willpower to solve illness.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>YOUR NEXT ACTION</h3>
          <p>Make a decision that reflects your values rather than only your immediate desire.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Non-harming also includes how you treat yourself. Accountability is not
        the same as endlessly punishing yourself through shame.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 / PRACTICE -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-yellow" id="paths-of-practice">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">06 / PATHS OF PRACTICE</p>
      <h2>FIND<br>YOUR<br>PRACTICE.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p class="ha-lead">
        Yoga is more than physical postures. Hindu traditions offer several paths
        for examining desire and living with intention.
      </p>
      <div class="ha-grid">
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>KARMA YOGA</h3>
          <p>Act responsibly without demanding a particular outcome.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>JNANA YOGA</h3>
          <p>Investigate the beliefs and attachments influencing your decisions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>BHAKTI YOGA</h3>
          <p>Explore devotion, surrender, and your relationship with the sacred.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="ha-box">
          <h3>RETURNING TO PRACTICE</h3>
          <p>Practice awareness, follow treatment, and accept support when you need it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Detachment doesn't mean you stop caring. It means you can want relief,
        connection, or forgiveness without allowing that desire to control your behavior.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 07 / ALCOHOL DIARIES -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-black" id="alcohol-diaries">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">07 / THE ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>THREE<br>STORIES.<br>ONE LENS.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p class="ha-lead">
        Desire looks different in each story, but responsibility remains part of all three.
      </p>
      <div class="ha-callout">RELIEF. DESIRE.<br>CONSEQUENCE.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 08 / FIRST DUI -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-yellow" id="first-dui">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">08 / ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>FIRST<br>DUI.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p class="ha-lead">Attachment to relief can turn into attachment to alcohol.</p>
      <p>
        Severe sciatica left you angry at your body and jealous of someone twice
        your age who could still ride a bicycle. You wanted the physical freedom
        you believed you should have at twenty-nine. Alcohol gave you relief when
        nothing else seemed to work, and you clung to it as though it were saving
        you. Through a Hindu lens, attachment to relief began to dominate your
        decisions. <em>Ahimsa</em>, or non-harming, asks you to recognize that
        drinking and driving harmed and endangered others, even though you were
        originally trying to ease your own suffering.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 09 / THIRD DUI -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-purple" id="third-dui">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">09 / ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>THIRD<br>DUI.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p class="ha-lead">Desire and responsibility can point in opposite directions.</p>
      <p>
        You were manic and impulsive, but you were also deeply attached to drinking.
        When the interlock wasn't functioning as expected, <em>Jackpot</em> meant
        an opportunity to drink; <em>No</em> was the judgment you ignored. Bipolar
        disorder affected your ability to decide, but you still recognize the
        actions as yours. Through <em>dharma</em>, you can acknowledge the illness
        without dismissing the consequences, and make amends through sobriety,
        treatment, and meeting your responsibilities.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 10 / PRESS SEND -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-pink" id="press-send">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">10 / ALCOHOL DIARIES</p>
      <h2>PRESS<br>SEND.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p class="ha-lead">Wanting connection does not entitle you to someone's response.</p>
      <p>
        When you were hurting, you wanted reassurance and communication immediately.
        Sometimes you sent messages intending to make another person feel the pain
        you felt. Your attachment to being heard became behavior that damaged
        relationships. <em>Ahimsa</em> asks you to acknowledge the harm caused by
        your words. Detachment asks you to apologize without demanding forgiveness
        and to let someone go their own way if that is what they want.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 11 / THE BIGGER PICTURE -->
  <section class="ha-section ha-black" id="the-bigger-picture">
    <div class="ha-heading">
      <p class="ha-number">11 / THE BIGGER PICTURE</p>
      <h2>WHAT<br>ARE YOU<br>CLINGING TO?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-copy">
      <p>
        Across these stories, attachment took different forms: relief from physical
        pain, the opportunity to drink, and immediate emotional reassurance.
        Desire narrowed your attention until what you wanted in the moment became
        more important than what followed. Hindu teachings on karma, dharma, and
        ahimsa help you examine the consequences without denying the suffering or
        illness that influenced your behavior.
      </p>
      <p>
        You cannot change the past or control whether other people forgive you.
        You can examine your attachments, take responsibility for your actions,
        and continue practicing discernment. Your history matters, but it does
        not have to be the entirety of your identity.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="hinduism-addiction-ending">
    <div class="ha-ending-heading">
      <p class="ha-ending-small">REFLECTION. DISCERNMENT. PARTICIPATION.</p>
      <h2>KEEP<br>ACTING<br><span>WITH CARE.</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="ha-ending-copy">
      <p>
        Wanting relief is understandable. It doesn't eliminate responsibility for
        how you seek it. Recognize what guides your actions, acknowledge the harm
        you caused, and make your next decision with care.
      </p>
      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION PERSPECTIVES",
          addiction_perspectives_path,
          class: "ha-back-button" %>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
